Because the "thunk" in the former case fits nicely
and comprehensibly on a single line. I'm not
ambitious to set some Guiness world record for
wasted whitespace, the all and only goal is to
achieve ease of understanding when read. So, what
needs stacking for clarity gets stacked, and what
doesn't, does not.

There's no style rule rigidity involved.

In a similar mode, I usually stack function
parameters:

   foo
   (
      parma,
      parmb,
      parmc,
      parmd
    )

based on _years_ of pain trying to pick out a single
parameter by eye from a jammed together horizontal
list, and all the time wasted as a result of missing
seeing one, but I have no religion involved in this
beyond the search for clarity. If the parameter list
is short and fits on a single line, so that there is
next to no chance of missing seeing a parameter, I
have no problem at all writing:

    foo( parma, parmb )

It helps to remember that I've been coding since
1961, more than enough time to learn by painful
experience that terseness in coding is _the_ mortal
enemy of code maintainability, an urge to be
resisted with every bit of the coder's will-power.

Or, as I put it in comp.lang.java.advocacy a year
or so back (roughly):

  faster typing skills trump terser coding skills

A contributing factor to all this is that I have
pretty profound astigmatism, and reading long
horizontal lines of type not offset by whitespace
is a physically painful, and a very error prone,
experience for me.  Thus both my narrow news
articles, and my frequent proofreading gaffes.

You cannot blame the gaffes on the slick text
editor, vim(), in which I format most of my postings
before pasting them to the idiotic editing interface
browser news access can provide. I'm just old and
miss a lot of errors these days from mental lapses.

